    "title": "Iran-Oman Route 'Agreed To' as Iran Warns Against US 'Interference'",
    "text": "• Iran and Oman have finalized a shipping route through the Strait of Hormuz.\n• The route is expected to be valid for 2-4 months.\n• Iran would send ships, including oil tankers, through the waterway to ensure it is free of mines before the strait opens to other shipping.\n• Ships would not be charged fees during the temporary arrangement.\n• The US would lift its naval blockade on Iranian ports and reinstate a waiver to allow Iran to sell oil and petroleum-related products if a deal holds.\n• Iran is aiming to \"stabilize\" its control over the Strait of Hormuz, which is its \"biggest military achievement of the war\".\n• Iran wants to ensure that there will be innocent passage, but not hazardous passage or war.\n• The US and its allies would not be allowed to transport military equipment that could be used for attacks against Iran.\n• A draft outline of the deal includes Iran coordinating inbound traffic and Oman managing outbound traffic.\n• The revenue from a \"service fee\" would be split between Iran and Oman.\n• The parties would work on clearing naval mines from the median lane of the strait within 30 days.\n• The deal is expected to be announced within the next few days.\n• The US has not officially confirmed its involvement in the talks, but a US official and a regional source said Iranian leadership completed its approval process on Tuesday.\n• The deal would be seen as a victory for Iran, leaving it with greater control over energy transit than before the war.\n• The US would essentially be cutting and running to extricate American forces from the deepening quagmire.\n• The deal would come at a high price, ratifying Tehran's control over the Strait of Hormuz.",
